---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const pageTitle = "Interior Finish Options | Appalachian Sheds Inc.";
const pageDescription = "A weather-tight shell does not include a finished room.";
---
<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 08</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 08 \u00b7 Comfort &amp; Utilities</div><h1>Interior Finish Options</h1><p>A weather-tight shell does not include a finished room.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Durable utility interior</td><td>$4,000\u2013$9,000+</td><td>whole project, historical</td></tr><tr><td>Finished office/studio interior</td><td>$6,500\u2013$15,000+</td><td>whole project, historical</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section><section class=\"asi-option-mini-gallery\"><h2>Visual comparison \u2014 planned photographs</h2><p>Images will be added where they help explain actual differences. No placeholder is represented as an installed product or completed ASI project.</p><div class=\"asi-mini-grid\"><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Overall appearance</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Close-up detail</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Installed scale / context</div></div></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>A 10\u00d712 shed does not contain only 120 square feet of finish work. Four walls, gable areas and the ceiling can easily create several times that much finish surface. Appalachian Sheds therefore prices wall and ceiling materials by actual installed surface area, then adds trim, coatings, flooring-readiness and detail work according to the selected package.</p><p>Best for workshops, tool rooms, garden buildings and active storage where wall strength, cleanability and durability matter more than residential appearance.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>Interior Finish Pricing Should Be Based on the Actual Wall and Ceiling Surfaces \u2014 Not the Shed Floor Area Alone.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A 10\u00d712 shed does not contain only 120 square feet of finish work. Four walls, gable areas and the ceiling can easily create several times that much finish surface. Appalachian Sheds therefore prices wall and ceiling materials by actual installed surface area, then adds trim, coatings, flooring-readiness and detail work according to the selected package.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Durable Utility Interior</h3><p>Best for workshops, tool rooms, garden buildings and active storage where wall strength, cleanability and durability matter more than residential appearance.</p><p>Typical scope: plywood or durable utility panels, sealed / painted surfaces, simple trim, electrical coordination and floor-finish readiness.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Office &amp; Studio Interior</h3><p>Best for backyard offices, studios, hobby rooms and clean flex spaces where a smoother, room-like interior is desired.</p><p>Typical scope: drywall or smooth panel walls, painted ceiling, trim, paint, lighting coordination, flooring readiness and comfort-system coordination.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Appalachian Wood Interior</h3><p>Best for premium retreats, studios, pool houses and custom buildings where warm wood character and detailed finish work are central to the design.</p><p>Typical scope: pine T&amp;G, beadboard or mixed-material surfaces, upgraded trim, clear / painted finishes, accent details and coordinated lighting.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>Know What Each Finish Material Costs Before Building the Package</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The most accurate way to price a finished interior is to price each wall and ceiling system by actual surface area, then add trim and finish details separately.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Small Buildings Can Cost More Per Square Foot.</h3><p>Mobilization, corners, window and door casing, gable cuts, ceiling work and trim labor do not shrink in direct proportion to the shed footprint.</p><p>Finish-square-foot pricing refers to actual wall or ceiling area being covered, not the building footprint.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>The Finish Goes On Late \u2014 But the Finish Plan Starts Early</h3><p>Interior finish work should not begin until the shell, rough-ins and insulation strategy are ready. This prevents finished surfaces from being opened later to move wiring, add blocking or correct comfort problems.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Rough-In First. Finish Last.</h3><p>The most expensive interior mistakes are usually sequencing mistakes rather than material mistakes.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>The Best Interior Is the One That Matches the Work Happening Inside</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A workshop and a backyard office should not receive the same wall system. Durability, sound, lighting, humidity, storage and appearance priorities are different.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Use Comes Before Appearance.</h3><p>Select the performance needs first, then choose the finish character.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Design the Interior Before We Cover the Framing</h3><p>Appalachian Sheds can coordinate the wall finish, ceiling finish, trim, lighting, storage, insulation, HVAC readiness and flooring plan so the finished space performs as well as it looks.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Decide whether the intended use requires a finished, conditioned or utility-served space. Record that use before pricing the shell and any separately scoped interior work.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">Interior Finish Pricing Should Be Based on the Actual Wall and Ceiling Surfaces \u2014 Not the Shed Floor Area Alone.</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">Know What Each Finish Material Costs Before Building the Package</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">The Best Interior Is the One That Matches the Work Happening Inside</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

